diff --git a/tests/nv2a_readback/README.md b/tests/nv2a_readback/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..19b1213
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/nv2a_readback/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+# Batched GPU readback regression
+
+The renderer ends one D3D11 EVENT query after all staging copies and submits the
+batch with one explicit `Flush`. An unfinished nonblocking poll calls
+`GetData(DONOTFLUSH)` once instead of repeatedly attempting to map every staging
+texture. It maps the real resources only after event completion; a busy Map
+still delays acknowledgement. Query allocation or GetData failures retain the
+existing Map fallback, and blocking staging-slot reuse keeps its original path.
+Pixel conversion, downsampling, format alpha and later CPU-row merge are intact.
+
+Microsoft documents EVENT completion as `S_OK` plus a true BOOL, and
+`DONOTFLUSH` as preventing implicit command-buffer flushes:
+
+- [D3D11_QUERY_EVENT](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11/ne-d3d11-d3d11_query)
+- [GetData](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11/nf-d3d11-id3d11devicecontext-getdata)
+- [D3D11_ASYNC_GETDATA_DONOTFLUSH](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11/ne-d3d11-d3d11_async_getdata_flag)
+
+Build after entering the game repository's `scripts/enter-env.ps1`:
+
+```powershell
+cmake -S tools/xboxrecomp/tests/nv2a_readback -B conformance_tmp/stutter-readback-test -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
+cmake --build conformance_tmp/stutter-readback-test
+ctest --test-dir conformance_tmp/stutter-readback-test --output-on-failure
+```
+
+Sixteen tests execute actual GPU copies and verify every logical guest pixel
+and pitch-padding byte. Four MSAA cases (1/2/4/8) cover an eight-surface batch,
+16/32-bit formats and late CPU rows. Controlled unfinished query responses prove
+that 64 polls issue exactly 64 GetData calls, zero Maps and no additional Flush;
+then normal GPU completion supplies the expected pixels. Other tests cover
+event allocation retry, GetData failure fallback, busy Map retry after real
+event completion, dropped/resized surfaces at reused addresses, and downsample
+view allocation failure retaining dirty pixels for a successful next-batch retry.
+
+`RECOMP_READBACK_POLL_US` controls readiness-query cadence, defaulting to 250
+microseconds when absent or empty. It accepts integer microseconds from 1 through
+1000; explicit `0` or invalid values select the original unthrottled polling.
+Startup reports the configured positive interval once. Repeated pending
+EVENT checks within that interval return busy without querying, mapping,
+flushing or acknowledging completion. Each new copy batch queries immediately,
+and blocking staging-slot reuse bypasses the gate. Unavailable or invalid clock
+readings permit immediate checks. No additional clocks run with the option off.
+The four cadence tests use a controlled clock around real staging copies to
+verify absent/empty defaults, explicit disable and invalid inputs, exact deadline
+behavior, retained guest pixels before completion,
+new-batch reset, blocking bypass and clock-failure fallback. This option has no
+universal performance guarantee; compare equivalent captures when tuning it.
+
+`nv2a_d3d_set_readback_poll_us(int us)` accepts runtime requests from 0 through
+1000 and returns 1, or rejects invalid values with 0. It publishes only an atomic
+request. The renderer applies it after completing the old batch and before
+issuing the next one; pending pixels and completion publication keep their old
+policy until then. `nv2a_d3d_get_readback_poll_us()` returns the effective interval,
+or -1 before the first batch. A control-thread regression switches 0 to 250 to 0
+around real copies, checks exact pixels and pending-batch isolation, validates
+invalid-request rejection, and verifies that disabling removes hot-path clock
+calls while re-enabling reuses the cached QPC frequency.
+
+The additional applied-scale test checks that the thread-safe
+`nv2a_d3d_get_render_scale()` returns -1 before renderer initialization and
+reports the renderer-applied value, retaining it while a new request is pending.
+
+`RECOMP_READBACK_FENCE=1` opts into native D3D11 fence readiness. It is disabled
+by default. The renderer probes Device5/Context4 and creates one fence, then
+signals a monotonically increasing value after each staging-copy batch, before
+the existing single Flush. Nonblocking completion reads GetCompletedValue at
+the same configured polling cadence. The EVENT query is still ended per batch
+for fallback if interfaces, fence creation, Signal or device removal fail.
+Blocking staging reuse, Map retries and guest CPU-row merge retain their paths.
+Dropping a batch clears its target without rewinding the fence sequence, so an
+old completion cannot finish a replacement batch. No Win32 event is allocated.
+The ten fence tests cover strict opt-in, cadence, real pixels and Map retries,
+abandoned targets, blocking reuse, both interface failures, allocation/Signal
+failure, removal sentinel and sequence exhaustion. Native-path tests require
+fence support on the test adapter. Performance benefit requires a paired game
+capture; this option does not reduce GPU rendering or copy work.
+
+- [CreateFence](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11_4/nf-d3d11_4-id3d11device5-createfence)
+- [Signal](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11_3/nf-d3d11_3-id3d11devicecontext4-signal)
+- [GetCompletedValue](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11_3/nf-d3d11_3-id3d11fence-getcompletedvalue)
+
+`nv2a_d3d_set_readback_fence(int enabled)` atomically requests0/1 and rejects
+other values. Only a new staging-copy batch applies the request, retaining the
+current batch's completion policy. `nv2a_d3d_get_readback_fence()` reports1 for
+native fences,0 for EVENT queries (including unsupported native requests), or
+-1 before the first batch. Disabled batches retain cached native interfaces.
+The control-thread runtime regression verifies0-to1-to0 pending-batch isolation,
+real pixels, blocking reuse and cached-interface reuse on re-enabling.
+
+The opt-in `RECOMP_HITCH_TRACE` pending marker reports `poll_count` and
+`max_poll_gap_ms`. Timing begins on the first unfinished poll, after deliberate
+vblank pacing. The maximum gap compares poll start times, so it exposes pauses
+in polling; it does not independently measure GPU execution time. Separate
+`readback_poll`/`readback_blocking` markers still measure slow CPU calls. Trace-off
+regression, with cadence explicitly disabled, verifies zero QPC calls during readback.
+When cadence is enabled, deferred checks do not increment `poll_count`; it counts
+actual readiness polls, and the measured gap includes deliberate query deferral.
+
+For individual calls above the hitch threshold, `readback_parts` separates
+query, mapping, pixel-copy and unmapping wall time, with surface/row counts.
+Measurements are buffered until the call finishes so diagnostic output is
+excluded from those subphases. These clocks are also trace-only. A long wall
+time can include thread preemption; use thread CPU time or sampling before
+attributing it to a blocking driver call.
+
+The optimization removes redundant readiness/driver calls. It does not remove
+GPU copy work or guarantee a pending batch cannot stall. A complete-frame timing
+claim needs a clean gameplay comparison without concurrent tests or builds.
